  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 PM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nDifficult forecast continues in regards to convection coverage\nand timing over the next couple of days. Will focus on the next\n24 hours or so as guidance seems to be flummoxed with this ring\nof fire pattern that is currently sitting over our forecast \narea. A stationary boundary continues to sit form west to east \nover central IA, northern IL, and into northern IN/southern MI. \nConvection has been firing along this boundary over the last day\nor so and looks to continue to do so. With pretty much a lack \nof any agreement in the different CAMs models uncertainty \nremains pretty high on the exact timing and coverage of any \ndeveloping convection. Currently a couple of thunderstorms have \ncontinued to form over northern IL and a couple of cells have \nalso popped up just to the southeast of our CWA. Watching for \npossible backbuilding northwest from the cells to our southeast \nand also further storm development eastward from the storms \nover northern IL. A very moist airmass is in place with surface \ndew points in the mid to upper 70s, PWATs also remain elevated \naround 1.75\". Instability is also abundant with SBCAPE values of\n5000 to 6000 J/kg present this afternoon into the evening. \nEffective bulk shear values around 40 kts will also be \navailable. The stationary boundary will most likely be the focal\npoint for initial storm development but with the amount of \ninstability present and this is what makes this forecast so \ndifficult is that any outflow boundary or weak disturbance is \nenough to initiate further storm development out away from the \nboundary. Pinning down where storms will and when will \nultimately develop continues to be the main quandry. However, \nany thunderstorms that develop will have all threats available \nespecially wind, hail, and heavy rainfall but with SR Helicity \nvalues around 100 m2/s2 and the close proximity of the boundary \nwill also allow for tornado spin ups. SPC still has our area \nunder a slight risk for severe weather tonight. \n\nOvernight tonight there will continue to be periods of\nthunderstorms pushing through the area in a more scattered\nnature but a few lingering stronger cells that develop this\nevening may continue to impact the area into the overnight\nperiod and even continue into the morning period on Tuesday.\nSome indications do having a brisk southward push of the \nboundary by Tuesday afternoon and this will increase the forcing\navailable and would help to move the activity into central \nIndiana, but again with models struggling in the near term \nreally do not have high confidence on any solution. SPC \ncurrently has the southwest half of our CWA in an enhanced risk \nof severe storms tomorrow. This does make sense with the better \nforcing and some models hint at a linear convective band pushing\nfrom the northwest parts of the CWA southward into the southern\nhalf of the CWA. Again, all threats look to be possible but the\nmain threats will be damaging winds and heavy rainfall on \nTuesday.\n\nWith the southward shift to the boundary the SPC is calling for\na marginal severe risk for our southwestern portions of the CWA\non Wednesday. With the still quasi zonal flow remaining in place\non the northern side of ridging over the southeast CONUS\ntemperatures look to remain seasonable through the weekend.\nStill taking the longer term with a grain of salt as the near\nterm evolution will lead to the longer term outcome.",
